Source package: glib2.0

scope-before-exec.patch
    Puts each application which GDesktopAppInfo launches into its own systemd scope before the
    application starts, app-gnome-<application ID>-<pid>.scope, so that every process of the
    application starts in that scope. GNOME Shell, gnome-session and gnome-settings-daemon asked
    systemd for the scope only once GLib had started the application, and systemd moves only the
    process it is given, so the processes the application started in the meantime stayed in the
    cgroup of the service which launched it, and were stopped with that service. Signal's zygotes
    and GPU process stayed in gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service, and Thunderbird's crash helper
    in org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service.

    GLib names the scope in the environment of gio-launch-desktop, which executes the application,
    and gio-launch-desktop asks systemd for the scope, for itself, and waits until /proc/self/cgroup
    names the scope before it executes the application. If it ran the application before that, every
    process the application starts until systemd moves it would stay outside of the scope, which the
    patch is there to prevent. gio-launch-desktop therefore waits as long as systemd works on the
    request: until the process is in the scope, systemd refused the request, or the job starting the
    scope ended with another result than done. When systemd is busy, an application therefore starts
    late, but whole. gio-launch-desktop gives up after 60 seconds, which systemd needs for a request
    only when it is badly stuck.

    The request comes from gio-launch-desktop, which stays connected to the session bus until
    systemd has answered, because systemd checks the caller of a request on the bus before it acts
    on it, and refuses the request of a caller which is gone by then, with ENXIO, which nobody then
    receives. A launching process which exits right after launching, as gio open behind xdg-open
    does, is often gone by then. gio-launch-desktop therefore links against GIO on Linux, for its
    D-Bus request.

    GLib asks for no scope where systemd would refuse it for certain, as gio-launch-desktop logs a
    refusal as an error: when systemd is not on the session bus, as on a test's dbus-run-session,
    which GLib checks with the bus before spawning, and when the name is longer than systemd
    accepts. An application starts about 24 ms later than without a scope: in a container, 33 ms
    instead of 9 ms passed from the launch call until the application ran.

    An application which runs outside of its scope after all is logged at error priority, which the
    daily journal digest reports: as "gio-launch-desktop: systemd did not create scope ...", with
    the reason systemd gave, as "gio-launch-desktop: Scope ... did not exist after 60 seconds", and
    as "gio-launch-desktop: Could not connect to the session bus to create scope ...". None is
    expected. A refusal with org.freedesktop.systemd1.TransactionIsDestructive, which systemd
    answers while it stops at the end of a session, is logged at notice priority. A journal stream
    gives every line the same priority, info for an application GNOME Shell launches, so when
    standard error is the journal, gio-launch-desktop logs through syslog instead, and otherwise to
    standard error.

    systemd looks up a process ID in its own PID namespace, so a process in a sandbox with a PID
    namespace of its own, such as Firejail's, which still uses the session bus, would name some
    other process. gio-launch-desktop therefore gives systemd its process as a pidfd, when the bus
    can pass one. gio-launch-desktop counts a cgroup below the scope's as in the scope, as Fedora's
    uresourced moves each process of an application into one.

    Processes started without GDesktopAppInfo are not covered by this patch. GNOME Shell's commands,
    run with Alt+F2 or by its spawn helpers, and gnome-settings-daemon's fallback Orca are therefore
    launched through GDesktopAppInfo as well, by patches/gnome-shell/spawn-commands-in-scope.patch
    and patches/gnome-settings-daemon/orca-fallback-in-scope.patch. Helpers which GNOME Shell spawns
    with pipes to talk to, like the authentication dialogs of VPN plugins, are not applications, and
    stay where they are.

    The scope is requested only for a process managed by the systemd user instance, and not in a
    Flatpak or a snap. The name is the one gnome-desktop's gnome_start_systemd_scope() gives the
    scope, so gnome-session's /usr/lib/systemd/user/app-gnome-.scope.d/ applies, and when a
    launching process asks for the scope too, systemd creates it for whichever request it receives
    first, with the same process, and refuses the other, about which gnome_start_systemd_scope()
    logs a warning when it is its own. The "launched" signal names the scope as "systemd-unit" in
    its platform data, which the patches of the same name in patches/gnome-shell/,
    patches/gnome-session/ and patches/gnome-settings-daemon/ look for to leave the scope to GLib.

    The approach follows https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/1596, without its
    shell wrapper, as gio-launch-desktop is back in the launch since GLib 2.86. GLib's developers
    prepared GDesktopAppInfo for scopes in https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/merge_requests/5350
    (merged after 2.88.0), with the scopes themselves announced as a follow-up. Drop this patch,
    and the three which depend on it, once GLib creates scopes itself, and check then which name and
    which platform data key it uses.

    GLib names the scope in g_desktop_app_info_launch_uris_with_spawn in gio/gdesktopappinfo.c, and
    the request and the wait are request_scope and wait_for_scope in gio/gio-launch-desktop.c, which
    gio/meson.build links against libgio on Linux. After a version bump, check that launching still
    spawns gio-launch-desktop and emits "launched" only after the spawn.

    The patch adds three tests to gio/tests/desktop-app-info.c, launch-desktop/scope-refused,
    scope-job-failed and scope-pending, which run gio-launch-desktop against a fake systemd on a
    test bus: gio-launch-desktop has to ask for the scope itself, run the application at once when
    systemd refuses the scope or fails its job, and keep waiting while the job runs. The build runs
    outside of a systemd user instance, so the scope itself is checked by
    tests/glib2.0/run-scope-test.sh, in a container with a user instance of its own, see
    tests/glib2.0/README.

    Check by hand after a rebuild and a new login:

      systemd-cgls --user-unit gnome-session-manager@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-session-service alone
      systemd-cgls --user-unit org.gnome.Shell@ubuntu.service
                                  should list gnome-shell, Xwayland and mutter-x11-frames alone,
                                  also after starting Thunderbird or Signal from the dock
      journalctl --user -b | grep 'Could not create transient scope'
                                  should find nothing

gsubprocess-trapped-accept-esrch.patch
    Fixes a race in the test /gsubprocess/exit-status/trapped of gio/tests/gsubprocess.c, which
    runs during the build. The test traces a helper with ptrace, which spawns a shell and kills it
    with SIGKILL as soon as the spawn returns, and asserts that continuing each stop of the traced
    processes with PTRACE_CONT succeeds. When the helper kills the shell after waitpid() reported a
    stop of the shell and before PTRACE_CONT, PTRACE_CONT fails with ESRCH, and with it the test and
    the build, with "assertion failed (ptrace (PTRACE_CONT, pid, NULL, 0) >= 0): errno 3: No such
    process". The patch makes the test accept ESRCH there. When the helper spawns true first, so
    that its threads exist before it spawns the shell, and the test sleeps 20 ms before PTRACE_CONT
    of processes other than the helper, the test fails this way every time without the patch, and
    passes with it. The patch changes only the test and is not proposed upstream yet. Drop it once
    Ubuntu ships a GLib whose trace_children() in gio/tests/gsubprocess.c accepts ESRCH from
    PTRACE_CONT.

executable-shell-quoting.patch
    Makes g_app_info_get_executable() return the executable of an application as GLib runs it: the
    first word of the Exec key as g_shell_parse_argv() splits it. It returned the first word up to a
    space, with the quotes of a quoted executable, and only the part before the space of one whose
    path has a space (GLib issue https://gitlab.gnome.org/GNOME/glib/-/issues/2930).
    g_app_info_create_from_commandline() names an application created without a name after the base
    name of the same word, which its FIXME suggested. A command line which g_shell_parse_argv()
    rejects, which g_app_info_create_from_commandline() accepts, still gives its first word up to a
    space.

    Firefox quotes the executable of a local handler in the command line it creates the handler
    from, see patches/firefox/local-handler-through-gio.patch, and scope-before-exec.patch names the
    scope of an application without a desktop ID after its executable, after the base name of an
    executable which starts with /. Without this patch, the executable of a handler started with a
    quote, so its scope was named after the whole quoted path, escaped, instead of
    app-gnome-handler-PID.scope.

    The fix is binary_from_exec in gio/gdesktopappinfo.c. The patch adds the test
    /appinfo/executable-quoted to gio/tests/appinfo.c, which fails without the fix. Not proposed
    upstream yet. Drop the patch once GLib fixes issue 2930.
